  "card": {
    "title": "Make all app notifications configurable (target: toast / in-app / system) via global or extension settings",
    "type": "feature",
    "scope": "# Summary\n\nMake every notification the app sends configurable, via both global settings and per-extension settings, and let the user choose which target(s) each notification is delivered to: toast, in-app, and/or system (OS-level) notification.\n\n# Context\n\nNotifications should not be hard-coded to a single delivery mechanism. Configuration should exist at two levels: global settings (app-wide defaults) and per-extension settings (overrides), with extension settings taking precedence over global. For each notification the user should be able to select any combination of toast, in-app, and system targets. The main work is introducing a shared dispatcher and migrating existing notification call sites to route through it.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Every notification routes through a shared, configurable dispatcher (no notification hard-coded to one mechanism)\n- [ ] Global settings let the user set default delivery target(s) for notifications\n- [ ] Extension settings can override notification targets per extension\n- [ ] The user can choose any combination of toast, in-app, and system targets\n- [ ] Precedence is defined and implemented: extension setting overrides global\n- [ ] Existing notification call sites are migrated to the dispatcher\n\n# Dev Hints\n\n* Introduce a shared notification abstraction/dispatcher that resolves the effective target(s) from global + extension settings before delivering.\n* Toast delivery today goes through `useToastStore` (`src/renderer/stores/toast.store`). \"System\" = OS-level notifications.\n* Align with Constitution Principle VII (error handling): errors must still surface as toasts regardless of configured targets.",
